    {
      "dom_id": "question-95283",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ has one negative singular value.\n\nII. Any $2\\times 4$ matrix $A$ has at most $4$ distinct singular values.\n\nIII. Any $6\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $6$ distinct singular values.",
      "worked_solution": "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false.  All singular values of any matrix are non-negative. So, \n$A$  can't have any negative singular value.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,  since $A$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $4\\times 4$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{4\\times 2}\\times {A}_{2\\times 4}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{4\\times 4}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $4$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is a $6\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 6$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 6}\\times {A}_{6\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
